-- Passage rg8-context-clues-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1c44f6ff-cb9f-57e6-8e1d-3b3065baea3f', 'rg8-context-clues-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Fighting Sound with Sound$q$, $q$On a crowded bus, you hear a constant mix of engine rumble, chatter, and traffic. This ambient noise, the background sound that surrounds you wherever you are, can make it hard to concentrate or even to hear your own music. Noise-canceling headphones are designed to solve that problem.

Sound travels as waves of air pressure, with high points called peaks and low points called troughs. Tiny microphones on the outside of the headphones listen to the noise around you. A small computer chip then creates a new sound wave that is the exact opposite of the noise, with a trough wherever the noise has a peak.

When the two waves meet, they counteract each other. The peaks and troughs cancel out, much like adding positive five and negative five to get zero. As a result, much of the steady rumble simply disappears before it reaches your ear.

This process happens almost instantly. The chip must analyze the noise and produce the opposite wave simultaneously, at the same moment the noise arrives, or the trick will not work. Even a tiny delay would let the original sound slip through.

The technology works best on low, steady sounds such as engines and fans. Sudden or changing sounds, like a dog barking or a friend calling your name, are harder to predict. With these sounds, the reduction is often negligible, so small that most listeners barely notice any difference.

Engineers first tested the idea decades ago. Their earliest prototype, a rough trial version built to see whether the idea could work at all, was heavy and awkward. Today, the same principle fits inside earbuds smaller than a grape.$q$, 'draft')
