-- Passage rg8-context-clues-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6f862461-14e4-5544-ad41-38e0c193cda5', 'rg8-context-clues-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Before Sunrise at Heron Marsh$q$, $q$Grandpa Teodoro believed that the best birdwatching happened before breakfast. So at five-thirty, Nadia followed him down the meandering trail into Heron Marsh. The path twisted left and right around ponds and fallen logs, and twice she was sure they had walked in a circle.

Grandpa was searching for one bird in particular, a tiny gray bird with a yellow throat that his birding club had been trying to find all summer. It was famously elusive. People had heard its song from the reeds a dozen times, but almost no one had managed to actually see it.

The reeds along the water were so dense that Nadia could not see more than a foot into them. Grandpa raised his binoculars anyway and waited.

The marsh was peaceful at that hour. The water lay perfectly still, a soft mist hung over the cattails, and the only sound was the distant call of a frog. Nadia felt her shoulders relax for the first time all week.

"Listen," Grandpa whispered. Nadia heard nothing. The song must have been nearly inaudible, too faint for her ears to catch, because Grandpa was already pointing.

Then she saw it. A small bird hopped onto a bare branch above the reeds. Against the gray sky and brown stems, its yellow throat was conspicuous, as easy to notice as a lit window at night.

"We spotted it," Grandpa breathed, and Nadia grinned. She knew they would be telling the birding club about this morning for weeks.$q$, 'draft')
