insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5926b490-2222-522a-9061-0bf4c852108e', 'rg8-evidence-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How Headphones Erase Noise$q$, $q$Sound travels through the air as waves of pressure. When a bus engine rumbles, it pushes air molecules together and pulls them apart many times each second. Your eardrum feels those changes in pressure, and your brain hears them as sound.

Noise-canceling headphones use this fact in a clever way. A tiny microphone on the outside of each ear cup listens to the sound around you. A small computer chip inside the headphones studies that sound and creates a new wave that is its mirror image. Where the noise pushes air together, the new wave pulls it apart. When the two waves meet, they cancel each other out, and the noise becomes much quieter.

This process works best on steady, low sounds, such as the hum of an airplane engine or an air conditioner. These sounds repeat in a regular pattern, so the chip can predict them. Sudden or high sounds, like a dog's bark or a person talking, change too quickly. The headphones cannot match them perfectly, so some of that noise still gets through. For that reason, most designs also use thick padding that physically blocks sound from reaching the ear.

Engineers who design these headphones face a careful balance. Cancel too little, and the listener still hears the engine. Cancel too much, and a walker might not hear a car horn or a bicycle bell. Some newer models include a setting that lets outside sounds through on purpose, so the listener can stay aware of the street.$q$, 'draft')
