-- Passage rg8-evidence-inference-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('907d3804-cc16-5957-b6c5-115b7eb8ad05', 'rg8-evidence-inference-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Bringing an Old Mural Back$q$, $q$For forty years, a mural of a busy harbor covered the side of the Hollis Street bakery in the town of Granger. Painted by local artist Rosa Delacruz, it showed fishing boats, gulls, and dockworkers unloading crates. Over time, sun and rain faded the colors until the boats looked gray and the sky was nearly white.

Last year, the town hired a team of art conservators to restore the mural. A conservator's job is different from a painter's. Instead of adding new ideas, conservators try to bring back what the original artist made. Before touching the wall, the team studied old photographs of the mural from local newspapers and family albums. They also talked with neighbors who remembered how the mural looked when it was new.

The photographs revealed a surprise. A small red lighthouse had once stood at the far left edge of the painting. It had faded so completely that no one under thirty remembered it. The team found faint traces of red paint in that spot and repainted the lighthouse using the photographs as a guide.

The work took three months. The team cleaned the wall with soft brushes and gentle soap, filled cracks, and matched each color by mixing small test samples. They chose paints made to resist fading in sunlight. Finally, they coated the mural with a clear protective layer that can be removed and replaced in the future without harming the paint underneath.

When the scaffolding came down, several older residents stood across the street for a long time. One man pointed to the lighthouse and told his granddaughter he had forgotten all about it.$q$, 'draft')
