-- Passage rg8-evidence-inference-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2513d238-1109-5d0b-8818-c4d390d2e932', 'rg8-evidence-inference-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Open the Library This Summer$q$, $q$Every June, the doors of the Fairview Middle School library close, and they stay closed until late August. I believe this is a mistake. Our school should open the library at least two mornings a week during the summer.

First, many students lose reading skills over the summer break. Teachers here spend part of every September reviewing material that students knew in June. Students who read regularly during the summer tend to lose less. Yet the nearest public library is across the highway, and many families do not have a car available during the workday. The school library is within walking distance for most of us.

Second, the library offers more than books. It has computers, a printer, and quiet tables. Some students need these to work on summer assignments, apply for programs, or simply read in a calm space. Without the school library, those students have fewer options than classmates with computers at home.

Some people argue that opening the library would cost too much. That is a fair concern. However, the parent association has already offered to supply volunteers, and the library would only need to open for six hours each week. Our librarian could train the volunteers in June. The cost would mainly be electricity and air conditioning for a few hours.

Last spring, our student council asked eighth graders whether they would visit an open library over the summer. More than half said yes. That is a strong sign that students want this. Opening the library would show that our school cares about learning all year, not only from September to June.$q$, 'draft')
