   $q$The author writes, "A beaver's front teeth are its toolbox." What does this metaphor suggest?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The teeth must be replaced the way tools wear out.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The teeth are the main tools a beaver uses to build.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Beavers store small objects inside their mouths.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"Beavers carry a box of tools between their teeth.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The teeth are the main tools a beaver uses to build.$q$, $q$The teeth do the cutting that lets a beaver build a dam, just as tools let a person build. The passage says the teeth "never stop growing," so they are not replaced like worn tools.$q$, 'draft', '6bd74f3b-abac-5678-803e-68351084f0c7')
