   $q$Mrs. Petrosyan calls the pepper plant "slender," but Darnell says, "It's skinny." Why does he change the word?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Slender\" means tall, and he wants to point out the plant is short.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-denotation"},{"text":"\"Slender\" sounds graceful, but \"skinny\" admits the plant looks weak.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Skinny\" is more polite, so he wants to be kind to the plant.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-connotation"},{"text":"The two words mean opposite things, so she described it wrong.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-synonym-antonym"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Slender" sounds graceful, but "skinny" admits the plant looks weak.$q$, $q$Both words mean thin, but "slender" has a flattering connotation and "skinny" a less flattering one, and Darnell says "Let's be honest." The words are near synonyms, not opposites.$q$, 'draft', '5aeb9ff3-6716-500d-8fb7-b2db9eb52faf')
