-- Passage rg8-figurative-language-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('89b4504f-6945-5d63-a6e0-b236c3d2ed1f', 'rg8-figurative-language-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$A Postcard From a Butterfly$q$, $q$Every autumn, millions of monarch butterflies leave Canada and the northern United States and fly south toward central Mexico. Some travel nearly three thousand miles. For an insect that weighs less than a paper clip, the trip is an astonishing feat.

Scientists sometimes describe the migration route as a highway in the sky. Monarchs follow wind patterns and mountain ranges, and they stop at the same kinds of places year after year. Fields of blooming wildflowers serve as rest stops where the butterflies refuel on nectar.

How do they find the way? Researchers believe monarchs use the sun as a compass. They also seem to sense the Earth's magnetic field. No single butterfly makes the full round trip, though. The monarchs that reach Canada again the next summer are the grandchildren and great-grandchildren of the ones that flew south.

Students across North America help track this journey. In a program run by a university, volunteers place tiny stickers on the wings of monarchs. Each sticker has a code. When someone finds a tagged butterfly in Mexico, the code shows where it started.

Last fall, a seventh grader named Hana Whitfeather tagged a monarch in her backyard in Minnesota. Months later, researchers reported that her butterfly had been found in a forest in Mexico. Hana described the news as "a postcard from a butterfly." Her class hung a map on the wall and traced the long, invisible road the tiny traveler had flown.$q$, 'draft')
