-- Passage rg8-literary-structure-pov-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2366d6b8-56e8-5831-85db-6109c8562e2c', 'rg8-literary-structure-pov-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Beaver Who Asked for Shortcuts$q$, $q$A young beaver named Tull wanted to finish his dam before the first frost, but he did not want to work very hard. So he swam to the heron, who stood in the reeds.

"Heron," said Tull, "how can I build a dam quickly?"

"Use only the thickest branches," said the heron. "They block more water."

Tull dragged three thick branches into the stream. They were so heavy that he could not fit them together, and the current pushed them apart by noon.

Next he swam to the otter, who was sliding down the muddy bank.

"Otter," said Tull, "how can I build a dam quickly?"

"Skip the mud," said the otter. "Mud is slow and messy. Just stack the sticks."

Tull stacked his sticks without mud. By evening, water poured through every gap, and the stack floated away.

At last he swam to the old beaver at the bend of the stream. Her dam was the largest in the valley.

"Grandmother," said Tull, "how can I build a dam quickly?"

The old beaver did not answer. Instead, she handed him a single small stick and a paw full of mud. "Place this," she said. "Then come back tomorrow and place another."

Tull grumbled, but he did as she said. Every day he placed sticks and packed mud, a little at a time. When the first frost silvered the reeds, his dam stood firm, and the pond behind it was still and deep.

Moral: The shortest path to a finished task is often the steady one.$q$, 'draft')
