-- Passage rg8-main-idea-summary-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b33dd340-960f-5d25-87e2-fafaea7f3f4e', 'rg8-main-idea-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Tool Library$q$, $q$Most households own a drill that gets used for only a few minutes each year. It sits in a drawer or a garage, waiting for the next shelf to hang. In a growing number of neighborhoods, people have decided there is a better way. They have started tool libraries, where members borrow equipment the same way they would borrow a book.

A tool library works on a simple plan. Neighbors donate tools they rarely use, or the group buys them with small membership fees. Members can then check out a ladder, a saw or a set of garden shears for a week at a time. Volunteers keep track of what has been borrowed and make small repairs when something breaks.

The benefits reach beyond saving money. When fewer people buy tools that sit unused, fewer tools end up in landfills. Many tool libraries also hold free workshops on fixing a bike tire or building a planter box, so members learn skills along with borrowing equipment.

Tool libraries can also bring neighbors together. In one city neighborhood, a retired carpenter named Mr. Delacroix volunteers every Saturday. Teenagers who come in to borrow a sander often stay to ask him for advice, and some have joined him to repair benches at the local park.

Running a tool library is not always easy. Volunteers must find a space to store everything, and a few borrowers return items late or damaged. Still, organizers say these problems are small compared with what the community gains. A shared shelf of tools turns into a shared set of skills and friendships.$q$, 'draft')
