-- Passage rg8-main-idea-summary-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2d755b3c-c817-5785-8e5f-91471a0fea16', 'rg8-main-idea-summary-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Engineers of the Stream$q$, $q$Few animals change their surroundings as much as the beaver does. By cutting down trees and piling up branches, mud and stones, a beaver family can turn a narrow stream into a wide pond. Scientists sometimes call beavers ecosystem engineers because their building projects reshape the land and the lives of many other creatures.

A beaver dam slows the flow of water. Behind it, a pond forms, and the ground around the pond becomes soggy wetland. These wet areas act like sponges. During heavy rain they soak up extra water, which can reduce flooding downstream. During dry spells they release water slowly, helping streams keep flowing.

The new ponds and wetlands also become homes. Frogs lay eggs in the calm water. Ducks and herons hunt for food along the edges. Young fish find shelter among the sunken branches, safe from strong currents. A stretch of stream that once supported a few kinds of animals may soon support dozens.

Beaver dams can cause trouble, too. Sometimes the rising water floods a road, a farm field or a backyard. In the past, landowners often removed beavers entirely. Today, some communities try a different approach. They install pipes through dams to control how high the water rises, letting the beavers stay while protecting nearby property.

Some land managers have even started bringing beavers back to areas where they had disappeared. They have found that a single beaver family can restore a damaged stream faster and more cheaply than human crews. The beaver's busy building, once seen mainly as a nuisance, is now valued as a natural tool for healthier waterways.$q$, 'draft')
