-- Passage rg8-text-structure-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('21104bb5-f87b-544b-8336-21203666d096', 'rg8-text-structure-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Our School Needs a Repair Cafe$q$, $q$Last month, my backpack zipper broke, and I almost threw the whole bag away. Instead, a classmate showed me how to replace the pull tab in five minutes. That small fix convinced me that our school should host a monthly repair cafe, where students bring broken items and learn to fix them together.

First, a repair cafe would save families money. Torn jackets, loose chair legs, and wobbly headphones are often tossed out even when the repair is simple. Fixing an item usually costs far less than replacing it.

Second, repairing things teaches skills that last. Students would practice measuring, sewing, and solving problems step by step. These are the same skills used in science labs and shop classes, so the cafe would support what we already learn.

Some people argue that students do not have time for another activity. That concern is fair, since many of us already have sports, clubs, and homework. However, a repair cafe could meet during the lunch period once a month, so no one would need to stay late. Students could also drop in for just ten minutes.

Finally, repairing reduces waste. Every item we fix is one less item in a landfill. If our school wants to teach responsibility, a repair cafe is a practical place to start.$q$, 'draft')
