-- Passage rg8-text-structure-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('52c4bad8-9e4f-5766-b1ff-f335540105e2', 'rg8-text-structure-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Sprinters and Distance Runners$q$, $q$At a track meet, the runners in the 100-meter dash and the runners in the 3,200-meter race may look alike in their uniforms and spikes. Inside their legs, however, their bodies have been trained for very different jobs. The difference comes down to two kinds of muscle fibers.

Fast-twitch fibers contract quickly and powerfully. They are ideal for short bursts, such as exploding out of the starting blocks. However, they tire within seconds because they burn fuel without much oxygen. Sprinters train these fibers with short, all-out efforts followed by long rests.

Slow-twitch fibers work in the opposite way. They contract with less force, but they use oxygen efficiently and can keep working for a long time. Distance runners rely on them to hold a steady pace lap after lap. Their training includes long runs at a comfortable speed, which helps these fibers grow more tiny blood vessels.

Everyone has both kinds of fibers, and the mix varies from person to person. Training cannot completely change that mix, but it can make each kind of fiber work better. This is why a coach watches how a new athlete performs before suggesting an event.

Both kinds of runners, then, are strong. They are simply strong in different ways, shaped by the fibers they use most.$q$, 'draft')
