-- Passage rg8-theme-summary-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('619559cb-4aeb-5a94-856c-38b5c72f3ffb', 'rg8-theme-summary-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-theme-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$What Happened$q$, $q$Ines had been sure about her science fair hypothesis since January. Bean plants that heard music every day, she predicted, would grow taller than plants kept in silence. She set up twelve pots on her bedroom windowsill, six with a small speaker and six without. Every morning she measured each plant with a ruler.

By the third week, the results were clear, and they were not what she wanted. The music plants averaged eleven centimeters. The silent plants averaged eleven and a half.

"Just leave out the two short ones," her cousin Dev suggested, pointing at the music group. "They probably got less sun anyway."

Ines looked at the two short plants for a long time. Dev might be right about the sun. But she had not written that down, and she had promised herself to report every measurement. She kept all twelve plants in her chart. Under the heading "Conclusion," she wrote that her hypothesis was not supported. Then she added a section called "What I Would Change," where she suggested rotating the pots so each one got the same light.

At the fair, her poster looked plain next to a volcano that actually smoked. A judge with reading glasses studied her chart and asked why she had not removed the short plants.

"Because then my data would say what I wanted," Ines said, "instead of what happened."

The judge wrote something on her clipboard. Ines did not win a ribbon that day. But before leaving, the judge stopped by again to ask if Ines planned to repeat the experiment next year with the pots rotated.$q$, 'draft')
