-- Passage rg8-theme-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('610b96a2-00a0-566e-ae29-cd3828380abe', 'rg8-theme-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-theme-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Wall on Juniper Street$q$, $q$Keoni had spent a month on the design. It showed Juniper Street from above: the corner market, the bus stop, the community garden, all in clean lines and careful shades of blue and green. When the community center approved it, he planned to paint every inch himself.

On the first painting day, though, neighbors kept showing up. Mrs. Lindqvist from the bakery wanted to paint the bakery's awning. Two brothers from the third floor asked for brushes. A girl named Amara, who was maybe six, wanted to paint the sun.

Keoni handed out brushes because he did not know how to say no. By noon, the awning was a brighter red than he had planned. The bus stop had a dog in it that was not in his sketch. Amara's sun was lopsided and much too orange, with rays that went in every direction.

That evening he stood alone in front of the wall with a can of yellow paint. He could fix the sun in ten minutes. He could make it match the drawing exactly.

He dipped the brush, then lowered it. He was thinking about Amara's face when she finished, the way she had stepped back and announced, "That's mine."

A month later, the mural was finished. Keoni noticed that people walking past did something he had not expected. They stopped and pointed, not at the whole design, but at small parts. "My uncle painted that bench," someone would say. "I did those flowers." Keoni found that he liked the lopsided sun best of all.$q$, 'draft')
